ID:LI Logging in to NetWare Networks from Multiple Windows
Quarterdeck Technical Note #194                       Filename: LOGIN.TEC
by Michael Bolton                                   CompuServe: LOGIN.TEC
Last revised: 10/22/93                                Category: NW

Subject:  A new feature in DESQview 2.61 greatly improves DESQview's support
          of Novell NetWare, and allows for multiple logins under DESQview.

Q. Can I log in to my Novell network from inside a DESQview window?

DESQview versions numbered 2.61 and later provide support for loading the
NetWare shell (NETX or EMSNETX) inside DESQview.  (Note that, as of this
writing, current versions of the DESQview manual contradict this more recent
technical document.)  This major new feature provides several signficant
benefits.

1) It allows for multiple NetWare logins inside DESQview; you may log in as
   the same user --  or as different users -- in different windows.

   * A network administrator might use a DESQview window to log in as
     the network supervisor from a machine on which she is troubleshooting
     for another user.

   * A software developer testing a multi-user application might like to test
     the program by logging in as multiple users from multiple windows.

   * You might like to set up a machine using remote access software, with
     multiple users calling in and logging in to the network separately but
     simultaneously.

   * An individual user might like to keep different environments, with
     different search maps, set up in multiple windows.

2) The NetWare shell need not be loaded before you start DESQview, but only
   in the windows in which you need access to your network.  Thus the shell's
   overhead is imposed only the windows in which you load it, and not on all
   the windows on the system.

In order to load the NetWare shell inside a DESQview window:

1) In Setup DESQview / Advanced Options / Network, make sure that you have
   set "Virtualize IPX/INT 7A" to Y.  Set a value of at least 8 in the
   "Network Buffers" field.

2) Load Novell's IPX (or, if you are using Novell's ODI drivers, IPXODI)
   before you start DESQview.  Do NOT load the NetWare shell (NETX or EMSNETX)
   before starting DESQview; the feature will not work if you do.

3) Load NETX or EMSNETX inside the windows for which you need network services
   and log in from inside those windows.  As usual, you will have to enter
   your name (and a password) at login time.  You may then use applications
   that reside on the network from inside those windows.  Note that you may
   have a different set of virtual drives and search maps within each of these
   windows.  Windows in which the shell is not running will not be able to use
   network file or print services.
   

   It is convenient to automate the process of logging in and loading an
   application via a batch file, and making that batch file the "Program" line
   in the DESQview Change a Program Menu.  Here is a simple example of such a
   batch file:

   REM WPNET.BAT
   REM Batch file for loading the NetWare Shell, logging in, and starting
   REM WordPerfect.  Assume that WordPerfect is in the APPS\WP directory on
   REM the VENUS file server, and that our user is Charlie Brown, whose
   REM username is cbrown.  He wants the WordPerfect logical drive to be
   REM drive W:
   REM
   EMSNETX
   LOGIN CBROWN
   MAP W:=VENUS/SYS:\APPS\WP
   W:
   WP
   LOGOUT
   REM End of batch file
 

   In this example, WPNET.BAT would be on the Program line of the Change a
   Program menu for the network WordPerfect window.

Additional Notes 
================

- You may still set up DESQview for your network in the traditional way,
  loading IPX and the shell before DESQview, and logging in before you start
  DESQview.  You will not be able to log in within a window, but you will have
  access to all network services in all windows.

- If you are in a situation where memory is at a premium, you should consider
  using EMSNETX rather than NETX.  Since it uses the expanded memory page
  frame, EMSNETX's overhead is only 10K, whereas NETX is substantially larger.
  EMSNETX is fully compatible with QEMM and DESQview.

- If you are using EMSNETX, make sure that you have not set Maximum
  EMS/XMS/VCPI/DPMI to 0 on the second page of the Change a Program Menu.
  Without at least 64K of EMS, EMSNETX will fail to load.

- The overhead for your network software can be reduced by specifying one of
  two parameters to IPXODI.

  IPXODI d can save 4K of memory by not loading the Remote Diagnostics
  Responder.  This portion of IPXODI is needed for the Novell utilities such
  as RCONSOLE and NVER, and for third-party network diagnostic programs, but
  is not needed otherwise.  

  IPXODI a saves 8K by disabling the Remote Diagnostics Responder and SPX
  support.  SPX provides support for applications that use NetWare's
  peer-to-peer protocols, including some print and diagnostic functions.  Most
  users should be able to take advantage of IPXODI a for the full 8K memory
  savings; network administrators may prefer to leave the diagnostic features
  enabled by using IPXODI a, or by using IPXODI without a parameter.

  
  Please see your Novell documentation for the benefits and liabilities of
  using these parameters.

- Software "sockets" are used to establish connections between machines.  By
  default, Netware's IPX provides 20 sockets, of which 4 are immediately in
  use.  Each network shell needs three sockets to initialize, so if you are
  using the default settings, your sixth shell will get a failure trying to
  allocate its sockets and will print out the message "A File Server could
  not be found".  If you need more than five separate logins from a single
  machine, increase the number of IPX sockets by creating a SHELL.CFG file in
  the directory from which IPXODI is run with the line IPX SOCKETS=nnn in it,
  where nnn is

  (the number of logins you desire times 3) plus 4

  If you are already using a SHELL.CFG, the line IPX SOCKETS=nnn may be added
  to it.

- Starting with DESQview version 2.6, DESQview can be run on a machine that is
  also configured as a NetWare Lite server.  The NetWare Lite software
  (CLIENT.EXE, NET.EXE) must be run before you start DESQview, and may not
  be run in a window.

  If you're on a network that incorporates both NetWare and NetWare Lite
  servers, you may still run NETX or EMSNETX and log in to a dedicated NetWare
  server within a window, AND run the peer-to-peer server software on your
  machine at the same time.  Again, it is not possible to run CLIENT.EXE and
  NET.EXE from within a window, but they may be loaded before DESQview.

